  <preface xml:id="preface">
    <p>
      We tend to miss out on the incredible, living chemistry unfolding right
      under our noses.
      When seen from the right angle, your home is a perfect demonstration lab
      of a myriad of chemistry concepts.
      Every time you wash your hands after eating buttered popcorn, fry an egg,
      iron a shirt, brush your teeth, or bleach a stain, you are executing
      complex chemical reactions and manipulating physical forces.
    </p>

    <p>
      I have specifically written this text for someone with zero background in
      science.
      No prior knowledge in chemistry is assumed, and I assure you that there
      will not be any dense pages of derivations or reaction schemes.
      Instead, I ask of you a curiosity about the various processes happening
      around you <mdash/> why water alone cannot wash off grease; why
      conditioner makes hair feel silky; why steak browns while boiling meat
      turns gray.
    </p>

    <p>
      But this does not mean I am dumbing down anything or avoiding complex
      topics.
      You will encounter molecular orbital theory, spectroscopy, protein
      analysis, and other topics typically learned by chemistry majors.
      I will try to gently introduce these topics using a intuitive physical model, and slowly level up our understanding as needed.
    </p>
  </preface>
</frontmatter>
